Q: SwiftUI GridRow background A: <p>I am providing this workaround as a guide to others that may find this code useful. I'm not marking this as a solution to this question.  This works for me in iOS 17.7 iPad simulator.</p>
<p>The idea is to set horizontalSpacing to 0 on the grid, and then manually set leading padding on each cell view in the row when <code>j &gt; 0</code>, and then set the background of each cell view.</p>
<pre><code>    func grid(alignment: Alignment = .center, h: CGFloat? = nil, v: CGFloat? = nil, striped: Color? = nil, keyStyle: TextStyle? = nil, keyAlignment: UnitPoint = .leading, valueStyle: TextStyle? = nil, valueAlignment: UnitPoint = .trailing, nilValue: String = &quot;-&quot;, from rows: GridEntries, tap: ( (Int) -&gt; Void)? = nil) -&gt; some View {
        Grid(alignment: alignment, horizontalSpacing: 0, verticalSpacing: v) {
            rows.views { [weak self] i,ENTRY in
                GridRow {
                    ENTRY.views { j,CELL in
                        Text(CELL ?? nilValue)
                            .gridCellAnchor(j == 0 ? keyAlignment : valueAlignment)
                            .padding(leading: j &gt; 0 ? h ?? 0 : 0)
                            .background {
                                if let striped {
                                    Group {
                                        i.isOdd ? striped : Color.clear
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                    }
                }
//                .background {
//                    if let striped {
//                        Group {
//                            i.isOdd ? striped : Color.clear
//                        }
//                        .frame(width: 999)
//                    }
//                }
                .backgroundAlmostTransparent
                .onTapGesture {
                    tap?(i)
                }
            }
        }
//        .clipped()
}
</code></pre>

Q: gcc 13.3.0: [-Wstringop-overflow=] A: <p>This is a well-known and confusing GCC warning.</p>
<p>The <code>st_size</code> field of the <code>stat</code> structure is typically of type <code>off_t</code>, a signed integer type. It can store negative values that become huge unsigned values that exceed <code>PTRDIFF_MAX</code>. GCC tries to warn about that. The suppression is simple, compare the value against the constant:</p>
<pre><code>if (0 &lt;= fi.st_size &amp;&amp; fi.st_size &lt; PTRDIFF_MAX) {
  htt = calloc(1, fi.st_size + 1);
  r = read(fd, htt, fi.st_size);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Conditional logic in a longitudinal survey A: <p>First, calc fields may only return numeric values, hence you getting field values rather than labels in your result. There is currently no neat way in REDCap to return the label of a selected value (nested ifs for each value is basically what you have to resort to).</p>
<p>Fortunately, I don't think this is a problem in your scenario where you are looking to <em>display</em> the value from either Form A or from Form B. Set up pairs of descriptive text fields, one for the Form A field and one for the Form B field, then use branching logic to show either one or the other, as required.</p>
